    "perplexity": {
      "description": "In It for the Money is Supergrass’s second studio album, released in 1997 on Parlophone. It finds the band broadening the scrappy exuberance of their debut into a more ambitious blend of Britpop, power pop, and classic British rock, with heavier riffs, sharper dynamics, and more varied arrangements. The record was widely seen as a confident step forward, showing a band that could balance youthful swagger with stronger songwriting and a more polished sense of structure.\n\nThe album’s sound is both punchy and playful, but it also has a slightly darker, more reflective edge than I Should Coco. Tracks move between explosive guitar energy, melodic hooks, and occasional psychedelic flourishes, helping the album feel bigger and more controlled without losing Supergrass’s sense of fun. It was well received on release and is often regarded as one of the band’s best albums, helping establish their reputation beyond the Britpop moment.",
